### What is the routing function?

Routing is the core function. The gateway maps an incoming path to a backend service using predicates.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class RoutingConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator bankRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
                .route("accounts", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
                        .uri("lb://account-service"))
                .route("payments", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/payments/**")
                        .uri("lb://payment-service"))
                .build();
    }
}
```

```text
// /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/balance → lb://account-service
// /api/v1/payments/transfer → lb://payment-service
// "lb://" means resolve via load balancer + service discovery
```

### How does authentication work at the gateway?

The gateway validates the JWT once at the edge and forwards a trusted identity context downstream. Services do not reimplement token checking.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.filter;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GlobalFilter;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Component
public class JwtAuthFilter implements GlobalFilter {

    private final JwtDecoder jwtDecoder;

    public JwtAuthFilter(JwtDecoder jwtDecoder) {
        this.jwtDecoder = jwtDecoder;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        String token = exchange.getRequest().getHeaders().getFirst("Authorization");
        if (token == null || !token.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }

        try {
            var claims = jwtDecoder.decode(token.substring(7));
            // Forward trusted identity downstream
            exchange.getRequest().mutate()
                    .header("X-User-Id", claims.getSubject())
                    .header("X-User-Roles", String.join(",", claims.getRoles()));
            return chain.filter(exchange);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }
    }
}
```

```text
// Request with Bearer token → gateway validates signature and expiry
// Valid: adds X-User-Id, X-User-Roles headers → forwards to service
// Invalid: returns 401 Unauthorized → service never sees the request
```

### How does rate limiting protect services?

Rate limiting counts requests per client and rejects excess traffic with HTTP 429. Spring Cloud Gateway uses a RequestRateLimiter filter backed by Redis.

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: payments
          uri: lb://payment-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/payments/**
          filters:
            - name: RequestRateLimiter
              args:
                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 100  # 100 tokens/second
                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 200   # burst up to 200
                key-resolver: "#{@userKeyResolver}"
```

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.ratelimit.KeyResolver;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Configuration
public class RateLimitConfig {

    @Bean
    public KeyResolver userKeyResolver() {
        // Rate limit per user (from JWT subject)
        return exchange -> Mono.justOrEmpty(
                exchange.getRequest().getHeaders().getFirst("X-User-Id"));
    }
}
```

```text
// User CUST-1001 sends 150 requests in 1 second:
// First 100: processed normally (replenish rate)
// Next 100: burst capacity allows up to 200 total
// Beyond 200: HTTP 429 Too Many Requests
```

### How does aggregation work?

Aggregation calls multiple services and merges responses into one payload. The client makes one call instead of many.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
public class DashboardAggregator {

    private final WebClient webClient;

    public DashboardAggregator(WebClient.Builder builder) {
        this.webClient = builder.build();
    }

    @GetMapping("/api/v1/dashboard/{customerId}")
    public Mono<Map<String, Object>> dashboard(@PathVariable String customerId) {
        Mono<Object> account = webClient.get()
                .uri("http://account-service/api/v1/accounts/{id}", customerId)
                .retrieve().bodyToMono(Object.class)
                .timeout(Duration.ofMillis(300))
                .onErrorReturn(Map.of("status", "unavailable"));

        Mono<Object> payments = webClient.get()
                .uri("http://payment-service/api/v1/payments/{id}/recent", customerId)
                .retrieve().bodyToMono(Object.class)
                .timeout(Duration.ofMillis(300))
                .onErrorReturn(Map.of("status", "unavailable"));

        return Mono.zip(account, payments)
                .map(t -> Map.of("account", t.getT1(), "payments", t.getT2()));
    }
}
```

```text
// Mobile app: 1 call to /api/v1/dashboard/CUST-1001
// Gateway: parallel calls to account-service + payment-service
// Response: merged {account: {...}, payments: [...]}
// Time: max(account latency, payment latency) = ~100ms
```

### How does fault tolerance protect the gateway?

Circuit breakers wrap downstream calls. When a service is down, the gateway fails fast with a fallback instead of hanging.

```java
// Route with circuit breaker and fallback:
// .filters(f -> f.circuitBreaker(cb -> cb
//         .setName("paymentCB")
//         .setFallbackUri("forward:/fallback/payments")))

// Payment Service healthy: request goes through normally
// Payment Service down: 5 failures → circuit OPEN → fallback in <1ms
// After 30s: HALF_OPEN → test call → success → CLOSED
```

### When should you use an API Gateway?

Use it when you have multiple microservices and multiple client types. It removes duplication of auth, rate limiting, and routing, and decouples clients from the internal layout.

```text
// Without gateway:
// Mobile app knows: account-service:8081, payment-service:8082, card-service:8083
// Each service implements: JWT validation, rate limiting, CORS, logging
// Client makes 3 calls for the dashboard

// With gateway:
// Mobile app knows: api-gateway:443 (one address)
// Gateway handles: JWT, rate limiting, CORS, logging (once)
// Client makes 1 call for the dashboard (gateway aggregates)
```

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

The most common mistake is putting business logic in the gateway.

```java
// WRONG: business logic in the gateway
@PostMapping("/api/v1/transfers")
public Mono<Object> transfer(@RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
    // Fee calculation in the gateway!
    java.math.BigDecimal fee = req.amount()
            .multiply(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.01"));
    // This is business logic that belongs in Payment Service
}

// CORRECT: gateway only routes
// Route: /api/v1/transfers → lb://payment-service
// Payment Service handles: fee calculation, balance check, transfer logic
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer keeps the gateway thin and horizontally scalable. Multiple gateway replicas sit behind a load balancer. For different client types, they use Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) with a tailored gateway per client.

```text
// Senior architecture:
//                    ┌─ Mobile BFF Gateway (lean payload)
// Load Balancer ────┤
//                    └─ Web BFF Gateway (rich payload)
//                         │
//              ┌──────────┼──────────┐
//          Account     Payment      Card
//          Service     Service     Service
```
